   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( f(x) = \\sqrt{x + 1} \\) and \\( g(x) = \\frac{x}{2} - 1 \\). The shaded region R shown is bounded by the graphs of f and g and the x-axis. By integrating with respect to y, find the exact area of R.",
      "command_verb": "find"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 9
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Solve each equation for x: \\( x = y^{2} - 1 \\) from the graph of f, with \\( y \\ge 0 \\), and \\( x = 2 y + 2 \\) from the graph of g.",
         "rule_named": "rewrite each curve as a function of y"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The curves meet where \\( y^{2} - 1 = 2 y + 2 \\), at \\( y = 3 \\), and the region runs from the x-axis, \\( y = 0 \\), up to there. On that interval the line is to the right of the parabola.",
         "rule_named": "limits in y and the right-hand curve"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The area is \\( \\int_{0}^{3} \\left(\\left(2 y + 2\\right) - \\left(y^{2} - 1\\right)\\right)\\,dy = 9 \\).",
         "mathjson": 9,
         "rule_named": "area integral in y"
      }
   ],
